/* book: the chapter, paragraph by paragraph (first sentences):
   - A PN sequence is a deterministic binary sequence that looks random.
   - This 3-stage register, started at 111, gives 1 1 1 0 0 1 0, period 2³ − 1 = 7.
   - Gp = Rc/Rb = Tb/Tc
   - the processing gain of DSSS, the number of chips per bit; in decibels Gp(dB) = 10 log10(Rc/Rb).
   - d(t) is the bipolar data (±1) at Rb and c(t) the bipolar PN code (±1) at Rc.
   - The receiver multiplies by the same carrier and by the same code, aligned to the chip.
   - A narrowband jammer at the carrier meets the code only once, at the receiver, so the multiplication spreads it over about 2Rc; the data filter then keeps only about 1/Gp of its power.
   - Mj = Gp(dB) − (Eb/N0)req(dB) − Lsys(dB)
   - Lsys is the implementation loss of the system, often taken as 0 in exam questions.
   - FHSS keeps a narrowband modulation (usually M-FSK) but the PN code drives a frequency synthesiser that picks one of N channels at each hop.
*/
