/* book Worked example 2: Q: The system above runs at fc = 2 GHz in a car at 108 km/h on a channel with στ = 0.2 µs. Is the fading slow or fast, flat or frequency-selective for the whole signal and for one subcarrier? Find Pb for BPSK at a mean Eb/N0 of 20 dB in Rayleigh fading, without and with two-branch MRC.
   1. v = 108/3.6 = 30 m/s; fd = 30 × 2 × 109/3 × 108 = 200 Hz; Tc ≈ 0.423/200 = 2.1 ms.
   2. T = 4 µs is about 530 times shorter than Tc: slow fading, H[k] stays put over hundreds of symbols.
   3. Bc ≈ 1/(5 × 0.2 µs) = 1 MHz. The 20 MHz signal is frequency-selective; a 312.5 kHz subcarrier is narrower than Bc, so it sees nearly flat fading.
   4. γ̄ = 100: Pb = ½[1 − √(100/101)] = 2.48 × 10−3 (≈ 1/400). In AWGN at 20 dB it would be about 10−45.
   5. Two-branch MRC: Pb = 1.84 × 10−5 (≈ 3/(16 × 100²) = 1.88 × 10−5), about 135 times better.
   Answer: Slow fading; frequency-selective over 20 MHz but flat per subcarrier; Pb = 2.48 × 10−3 without diversity and 1.84 × 10−5 with two-branch MRC. */
